  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe propose to use a multi-resolution genetic algorithm (GA) with local improvement to optimize h(x). This approach will help escape local optima by exploring the solution space with global search (GA) and refining promising candidates with local search (gradient projection). The central idea is to:\n- Use multiple resolutions simultaneously (168, 336, 672 samples)\n- Combine GA with gradient-based methods\n- Balance exploration/exploitation\n- Include fitness terms for smoothness and constraint satisfaction\n\nThe bottleneck in previous approaches is likely local minima, hence the GA's population-based nature will help overcome this. The algorithm should find a configuration with lower maximum overlap by:\n\na) Exploring diverse solutions through crossover and mutation\nb) Maintaining the integral constraint\nc) Using cheap fitness approximations early (scaled autocorrelation)\nd) Verifying fitness with exact correlation computation\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Domain: [0,2]\n- Representation: Piecewise-constant n-point samples (n in {336,672})\n- Objective: Minimize C5 = max_k [\u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx]\n- Constraints: \u2211h[i] * dx = 1, 0\u2264h[i]\u22641\n\nThe fitness evaluator f(h) = (\u2211h[i](1-h[j]))^T for cheap approximation, and the exact computation is:\ndx = 2/n\ncorr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\nC5_exact = (\u2211(corr[max(0,i)] * dx) for all lags i)\nThen maximize over the best C5\n\nWe implement:\n- Projection operator to enforce constraints (clipping and redistribution)\n- Smoothness term to avoid pathological solutions\n- Early stopping if previous record is approached\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n- Step 1: Input resolution n (multiple n tested simultaneously)\n- Step 2: Initialize population with random feasible candidates\n- Step 3: Evolution with three phases:\n  a) Selection: Tournament selection with replacement\n  b) Reproduction: Two-point crossover and Gaussian mutation\n  c) Repair: Project each offspring to satisfy 0\u2264h\u22641 and \u2211h*dx=1\n- Step 4: Parallel evaluation of fitness\n- Step 5: Apply local improvement (gradient projection) to top candidates\n- Step 6: Guided mutation based on fitness ranking\n- Step 7: Monitor for record breaking solutions\n\nKey operations:\n- Mutation: h_new = project((h + \u03c3\u00b7randn) for each point)\n- Crossover: Two-point crossover\n- Fitness evaluation: Use cheap approximation for ranking, exact only for record attempt\n\n4. Search choices and variations\nWe use:\n- Population size: 100\n- Mutation rate: 0.2 per dimension\n- Tournament size: 5\n- GA parameters: \n  - Crossover: uniform\n  - Mutation: uniform with small std dev (0.05)\n\nLocal improvement parameters:\n- Projection: 200 clip steps\n- Learning rate: 0.01\n- Steps: min(50, time_remaining)\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- For n=336: Each GA evaluation: O(n^2) operations (correlation)\n- Time estimate: \n  - Correlation: 0.1ms per n=336\n  - GA generation: 100 individuals (10ms)\n- Budget allocation:\n  - n=336: 610 seconds\n  - n=672: 500 seconds\n  - Local improvement: 50 seconds per candidate\n\nGuard against:\n- Timeouts: Check solver completion every 100ms\n- NaNs: Clamp values to [0,1]\n- Divergence: Track worst fitness improvement\n- Dimension changes: Use interpolation\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- After completion, reevaluate final h with exact correlation\n- Verify h satisfies 0\u2264h\u22641 and sum(h)*dx=1\n- If global best found, ensure record is improved\n- Return (h_final, c5, n_points)\n\nFallback procedure:\n- If timeout, return best candidate from n=336\n- If invalid parent, transform by linear interpolation\n- For fitness evaluation: \n  - Use exact computation only if cheap approximation matched (within 0.001*)\n  - Otherwise use approximation to avoid time waste\n\n*This hybrid approach should find a solution with C5 < 0.38085. The multiple resolutions and parallel evolution help avoid local minima while the exact computations ensure correctness.",
